      "t": "Руки уберите от меня!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Тихо, тихо, что вообще происходит?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "gost",
      "t": "Вы были совершенно правы.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "gost",
      "t": "Она воровка!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "elena",
      "t": "Что?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Отпустите её, это недоразумение.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "gost",
      "t": "Спасибо за бдительность.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "elena",
      "t": "Витя, объясни мне, что это значит!",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let's go back through their conversation line by line and unpack the Russian phrasing.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "gost",
      "t": "Стойте, отдайте сумку!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Stop, hand over the bag!' the guest anchors the line on 'stop', while 'bag' completes the thought.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "elena",
      "t": "Что такое, это моя сумка!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'What is going on, this is my bag!' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'what' and finishing on 'bag'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "gost",
      "t": "Охрана, отдайте мою сумку!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Security, give back my bag!' Notice how Russian expresses 'security' and 'bag' with crisp inflection rather than helper words. Chef Barinov answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Так, что случилось?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Alright, what happened?' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'so' and finishing on 'happened'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "elena",
      "t": "Руки уберите от меня!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Take your hands off me!' The meaning hinges on 'hands' working together with 'me' in spoken Russian.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Тихо, тихо, что вообще происходит?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Quiet, quiet, what on earth is happening?' Chef Barinov raises the pitch on 'is happening' to turn the phrase into a direct spoken question. the guest answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "gost",
      "t": "Вы были совершенно правы.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'You were completely right.' In Russian, the idea of 'you' flows naturally into 'right' without extra filler words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "gost",
      "t": "Она воровка!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'She is a thief!' The meaning hinges on 'she' working together with 'thief' in spoken Russian.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "elena",
      "t": "Что?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'What?' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'what' and finishing on 'what'. Chef Barinov answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Отпустите её, это недоразумение.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Let her go, this is a misunderstanding.' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'let go' and finishing on 'misunderstanding'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "gost",
      "t": "Спасибо за бдительность.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Thank you for your vigilance.' Notice how Russian expresses 'thank you' and 'vigilance' with crisp inflection rather than helper words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "elena",
      "t": "Витя, объясни мне, что это значит!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Vitya, explain to me what this means!' Notice how Russian expresses 'proper name' and 'so' with crisp inflection rather than helper words. Alright, let's hear the entire conversation one more time from start to finish.",
